9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: E

The circumflex accent (ˆ) has a long and somewhat messy history in French spelling reforms and practices from the Renaissance onward. Its attested uses include:

- marking the loss of a following /s/ (or other consonant) that created compensatory lengthening or a different vowel quality (H);
- signaling reductions of diphthongs or of vowels that were once in hiatus (G, I);
- distinguishing homophones (B);
- marking vowel-quality contrasts such as open vs. close or front vs. back (A);
- indicating etymological length distinctions that survived into the classical period (D);
- supplying a pseudo-Latin or “scholarly” appearance to particular lexical items (F);
- and appearing on certain /o/ reflexes of Latin or Greek words (C).

Because every one of the concrete functions listed in A–D and F–I is documented in the historical record, the choice that correctly answers “which has never been attested” is E.

(The only function that has never been systematically employed is none of the above; all of them occur.)

